---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import {
  toastActionTree,
  toastEmitScript,
  toastEmitTree,
  toastSimpleTree,
  toastStackScript,
  toastStackTree,
  toastStatusScript,
  toastStatusTree,
} from "../../../demos/toast";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));


const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla @skryensya/react`;






const stackCss = `/* Already ships in components/toast.css: it's here because
   it's the only part of the component that reads the DOM
   from CSS, and it's worth knowing where it comes from. */

/* Only the newest remains in flux, so the region
   takes the newest toast’s size. Those in the back come out of flow
   stretched to that same box: that is why the stack is read
   as an object and not as out-of-square cards. */
.sk-toast-region:not([data-stack="off"]) > .sk-alert:not(:last-child) {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  overflow: clip;
}

.sk-toast-region:not([data-stack="off"]) > .sk-alert {
  /* 0 for newest (last child), +1 for
     each one left behind. No counters in JS. */
  --sk-toast-depth: calc(sibling-count() - sibling-index());

  transform-origin: bottom center;
  translate: 0 calc(
    var(--sk-toast-depth) * var(--sk-toast-peek) * -1
  );
  scale: calc(1 - (var(--sk-toast-depth) * var(--sk-toast-shrink)));
  opacity: calc(3 - var(--sk-toast-depth));

  /* The depth again, now in time: that of
     The front comes out first, each one behind follows him. */
  transition-delay: calc(
    var(--sk-toast-depth) * var(--sk-toast-cascade)
  );
}

.sk-toast-region:not([data-stack="off"]):is(:hover, :focus-within)
  > .sk-alert {
  position: static;
  overflow: visible;
  translate: none;
  scale: none;
  opacity: 1;
}

/* The layout does not transition: when it stops being absolute, it
   They are in their row. Instead of encouraging the jump, it is covered
   with an arrival. The newest one does not participate: it did not move. */
.sk-toast-region:not([data-stack="off"]):is(:hover, :focus-within)
  > .sk-alert:not(:last-child) {
  animation: sk-toast-fan var(--motion-expand-duration)
    var(--motion-expand-easing)
    calc(var(--sk-toast-depth) * var(--sk-toast-cascade))
    backwards;
}

@keyframes sk-toast-fan {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    translate: 0 var(--sk-toast-peek);
  }
}`;



const vanilla = `import {
  initComponents,
} from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";
import { mountIcons } from "@skryensya/vanilla/icon";
import { lucideIcons } from "@skryensya/icons-lucide";

// Icons are authored as <span data-sk-icon> placeholders;
// mountIcons replaces them with the <svg> of the linked set.
mountIcons(document, lucideIcons);

await initComponents();`;
---

<Base title="Toast" description="Toast: transient feedback in a floating region. Same anatomy as Alert, with app life cycle.">
  <h1>Toast</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Toast communicates transient feedback in a floating region. The item is a <a href="/en/components/alert">Alert</a>, same markup, same tones, wrapped in <code>sk-toast-region</code> and in the toast life cycle (mount, dismiss, optional timeout).
  </p>

  <h2>After an action</h2>
  <p>
    This is the real case: a toast is no longer visible, it appears because something happened. What is stable
    In HTML there are three pieces: the control that triggers the action, a
    <code>sk-toast-region</code> <strong>empty</strong>, and a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> with the
    item. The app clones, inserts and remounts; <code>initComponents</code> is idempotent, so
    which only touches what was recently inserted.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    tree={toastEmitTree(t)}
    js={toastEmitScript}
    previewScript={toastEmitScript}
    label="Toast after an action"
    note="press the button"
    viewport="menu"
  />

  <h2>Simple</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tone="neutral"</code> (the default). In a toast, color should almost never be the main signal: it already floats and retreats. Neutral lets the text say the message without painting more urgency, and floats on a surface <strong>raised</strong> (<code>--color-bg-surface-raised</code> + <code>--elevation-raised</code>) to be read as raised chrome, not as a page panel. See the same criteria in <a href="/en/components/alert">Alert</a>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={toastSimpleTree(t)} label="Toast simple" viewport="menu" />

  <h2>Stateful</h2>
  <p>
    Status tones color the panel. <code>danger</code> is advertised as <code>role="alert"</code> (assertive); the rest as <code>role="status"</code> (polite).
  </p>
  <p>
    The first one does not have ✕: it has <code>data-timeout</code>, so it leaves alone. The other two ask for something from the person, a display to confirm and an error to read, and so they stay until they are closed. The rule of thumb: <strong>if the toast can close itself, you do not need ✕; if you cannot, the ✕ is required</strong>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    tree={toastStatusTree(t)}
    js={toastStatusScript}
    previewScript={toastStatusScript}
    label="Toast with status"
    viewport="overlay"
  />

  <h2>With action</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-alert__actions</code> is a single column: the recovery action and the close live there, in that order. A toast with an action does not have <code>data-timeout</code>, disappearing before someone can press “Undo” turns the action into a decoration.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={toastActionTree(t)} label="Toast with action" viewport="menu" />

  <h2>Stacking</h2>
  <p>
    Stacking is the <strong>default behavior</strong>, not an option to enable. Four separate toasts
    can consume a quarter of the screen, while a region with several toasts is a normal result of an
    app reporting what it did. The toasts overlap within the footprint of <strong>one</strong>: the
    newest is in front and earlier toasts peek out behind it. Hovering the stack—or moving keyboard
    focus into it—fans the stack back into a normal list.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <code>data-stack="off"</code> for the uncommon region whose <em>purpose is</em> to show several
    messages at once. The tone example above uses it because all three toasts must remain visible
    together rather than form a stack.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stack uses the newest toast's box. It is the only toast left in flow, so the region takes its
    size and every earlier toast is positioned against the same box. The result reads as one object
    rather than misaligned cards: a two-line toast behind a one-line toast cannot protrude beyond the
    shared outline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The effect uses one number: <strong>depth</strong>. It comes directly from the DOM:
    <code>sibling-count() - sibling-index()</code> is 0 for the newest toast and increases by one for
    each earlier toast. The app only adds and removes nodes; it never maintains an index. Opacity
    reaches zero at the fourth toast because <code>calc(3 - depth)</code> is clamped. Browsers without
    CSS sibling-index support keep the normal list shown above—an honest fallback.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same depth controls each toast's opening <strong>delay</strong> through
    <code>--sk-toast-cascade</code>. The front toast moves first and each toast behind it follows one
    beat later, so the stack unfolds instead of expanding all at once. Returning to flow changes
    <em>layout</em>, which cannot transition. Each toast therefore covers the jump with a short fade
    and upward movement into its new position. The newest toast does not animate because it never
    moved.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    tree={toastStackTree(t)}
    js={toastStackScript}
    previewScript={toastStackScript}
    label="Pile of toasts"
    note="Hover to open it"
    viewport="overlay"
  />
  <p>
    The JavaScript is the same as the first example, and the HTML too: clone, insert, mount and remove in <code>sk-dismiss</code>. There is nothing to add to stack. The hooks are from the region: <code>--sk-toast-peek</code> (how much each one sticks out), <code>--sk-toast-shrink</code> (how much each step back shrinks) and <code>--sk-toast-cascade</code> (how much they separate in time when opening). No toast knows it is in a stack.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={stackCss} lang="css" label="components/toast.css" />

  <h2>The closure is a Button</h2>
  <p>
    The ✕ is authored as <a href="/en/components/button">Button</a> icon-only in size <code>sm</code> (<code>data-size="sm" data-icon-only data-variant="ghost"</code>), not as an ad hoc Alert control. With that, it inherits what Button already resolves: the 32px face, the 44px hit target that <code>:: after</code> expands beyond that face, the hover and pressure state layer, and the focus. With no visible text, <code>aria-label</code> is required.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also preserves the <code>sk-alert__dismiss</code> part class: it is the hook that the enhancer looks for to wire the dismissal, the one that makes the glyph read the color of the tone (<code>currentColor</code>) instead of the accent that a normal ghost would paint, and the one that corrects the <strong>optical position</strong>. An icon-only ghost is almost all air: 16px of glyph in a 32px box, plus the panel inset, leave the ✕ floating in a gap. The part is pulled back by half an inset unit, so the <em>glyph</em> falls where the padding says the edge of the content is, without the click area losing a pixel.
  </p>

  <h2>The life cycle belongs to the app</h2>
  <p>
    Toast does not manage queues or persistence: those cycles belong to the application. The component provides the region, reuses Alert, announces the tone and warns when it wants to leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Vanilla, <code>data-sk-toast</code> on a <code>sk-alert</code> registers the native dismiss and announces the tone. <code>data-timeout</code> is optional; When it expires, it issues <code>sk-dismiss</code> (same as the ✕) with <code>detail.reason</code>, <code>"timeout"</code> or <code>"dismiss"</code>, in case the app distinguishes “it left alone” from “they closed it.”The component never removes the node: that is done by whoever put it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of each preview. The API rightly reflects Alert (<code>title</code>, <code>icon</code>, <code>actions</code>, <code>presentation</code>, <code>tone</code>) plus <code>timeout</code> and <code>onDismiss</code>. Without <code>tone</code>, it is neutral.
  </p>
</Base>
